WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.400
I like finding exceptions to rules.

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:05.710
The sort of things that expose assumptions
about how we think the world works,

00:00:05.710 --> 00:00:09.330
and that confuse any computer system that
isn't set up to accept them.

00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:11.620
Like: streets have names, right?

00:00:11.620 --> 00:00:14.550
Okay, sure, there are some people out there
who'll immediately go,

00:00:14.550 --> 00:00:16.850
ah, no, see, there are lots of streets
that don't have names,

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:18.640
in rural areas and the developing world,

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:22.040
or there are the roads that just have
a number or a letter.

00:00:22.040 --> 00:00:24.720
And some of you will go, oh, well, actually,
Japan.

00:00:24.720 --> 00:00:28.310
Most of Japan doesn't name its streets,
it names and numbers its city blocks,

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:30.460
and besides it's more complicated than that...

00:00:30.460 --> 00:00:34.170
But, okay. In a Western European nation
like Germany,

00:00:34.170 --> 00:00:37.149
in the centre of a large city like Mannheim,

00:00:37.149 --> 00:00:39.429
you'd expect there to be street names.

00:00:39.429 --> 00:00:42.559
Nah. Welcome to the exception to the rule.

00:00:42.559 --> 00:00:46.489
Here inside the Quadrates of Mannheim,
the Squares of the town centre,

00:00:46.489 --> 00:00:48.930
only a few major streets have names.

00:00:48.930 --> 00:00:53.129
Every other building is referred to
by which square it's on, as part of a grid.

00:00:53.129 --> 00:00:56.139
The west half gets the letters A-K;
the east half, L-U,

00:00:56.139 --> 00:00:59.679
and that's followed by the number of blocks
from the centre.

00:00:59.679 --> 00:01:04.350
So you can have C4, or in German "𝕮𝟜" [see-fear],

00:01:04.350 --> 00:01:07.909
which explains why the art gallery on it
is called Zephyr.

00:01:07.909 --> 00:01:11.060
Given the German reputation for humour,
that's a pretty good multilingual pun.

00:01:11.060 --> 00:01:14.409
None of the modern mapping companies know
what to do with this.

00:01:14.409 --> 00:01:17.350
They'd be fine if the whole country ran on
a different system,

00:01:17.350 --> 00:01:19.020
but mixing and matching in the same area,

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:22.359
in a region that, as far as most people know,
just doesn't do this...

00:01:22.359 --> 00:01:24.020
well, they can't cope.

00:01:24.020 --> 00:01:27.860
Google and Microsoft get confused and give
each road multiple names.

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:31.520
Apple insist that each road just has one name,
and they're entirely wrong,

00:01:31.520 --> 00:01:34.270
although it's Apple Maps, so, you know(!)

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:39.119
OpenStreetMap, which is community-led, recognises
full addresses but not the individual blocks,

00:01:39.119 --> 00:01:43.549
although presumably that'll be fixed within
a few hours of this video being uploaded.

00:01:43.549 --> 00:01:45.680
This isn't some modern invention, either.

00:01:45.680 --> 00:01:50.689
This isn't something brought on by future-looking
city planners rebuilding after the Second World War,

00:01:50.689 --> 00:01:52.280
or 80s planners getting ahead of themselves.

00:01:52.280 --> 00:01:55.299
While the numbering patterns have changed
over the years,

00:01:55.299 --> 00:01:59.530
this bit of Mannheim has had some form of
block numbering for a long, long time.

00:01:59.530 --> 00:02:04.369
Why change it just because some programmers
can't figure out how to put it into their database?

00:02:04.369 --> 00:02:08.250
The programmers have only been around for a
few years. This city has been around for centuries.

00:02:08.250 --> 00:02:13.000
The address of a place here in the Quadrates
really is just a number, then a block,

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.820
Mannheim, Germany.

00:02:14.820 --> 00:02:16.480
Even if some computers don't believe it.

